<p>Sudan marked the start of a fourth year of war on April 15, with outlets across the world describing a conflict between the army and the Rapid Support Forces that has brought famine, mass displacement, and allegations of atrocities. The United Nations and aid officials say it remains the world’s worst, or largest, humanitarian crisis. The fighting that began in 2023 has forced about 13 million people from their homes, with no clear end in sight, even as some parts of Khartoum show fragile signs of daily life returning. The latest development is a fresh international push for relief funding. Germany hosted a donor conference in Berlin on Wednesday, announcing another $30 million in aid, while Canada pledged $120 million. The conference raised about €1.3 billion for Sudan and neighboring countries as governments tried to bring attention back to a crisis overshadowed by other regional and global turmoil. At the same time, some reports say the worsening regional conflict in the Middle East is tightening fertilizer supplies and could deepen hunger inside Sudan, adding another strain to food access for civilians already surviving on extremely limited meals.</p><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Country divided: After three years of fighting, the war has effectively split Sudan into rival zones, with battles increasingly centered in the country’s middle regions, including Kordofan and the Nuba Mountains.</li><li>Household rationing: Semafor reported that millions of people across Sudan are surviving on just one meal a day, showing how the crisis is reaching into daily life at home.</li><li>Future erosion: Al Jazeera reported that the war has devastated civilian infrastructure and damaged Sudan’s education and health systems, eroding the country’s future.</li></ul><blockquote>We no longer ask what we will eat. We ask who will eat, - A person in North Darfur</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>German government: Foreign Minister Johann Wadephul said Berlin wanted donor pledges to top the roughly $1 billion raised at the previous London conference and signaled that more commitments were on the way. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/after-three-years-war-sudan-confronts-devastation-donors-gather-berlin'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Sudanese government: Sudan condemned the Berlin conference as &quot;surprising and unacceptable&quot; interference, signaling resistance to parts of the international diplomatic approach. (<a href='https://allafrica.com/stories/202604150354.html'>AllAfrica</a>)</li><li>Aid agencies and UN officials: Humanitarian officials described Sudan as an &quot;abandoned crisis&quot; and the world’s largest humanitarian challenge, urging donors and governments to refocus on civilian suffering. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/sudan-enters-fourth-year-war-officials-lament-abandoned-132051316'>ABC News</a>)</li><li>Sudanese civilians: Residents interviewed by outlets described feeling humiliated, broken, and powerless, while some in Khartoum also pointed to fragile signs of life returning despite the devastation. (<a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/15/nx-s1-5781032/sudan-darfur-war-genocide-famine'>NPR</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/africa/article/3350197/sudan-enters-4th-year-war-abandoned-crisis-grows'>Sudan enters 4th year of war as ‘abandoned crisis’ grows</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/15/nx-s1-5781032/sudan-darfur-war-genocide-famine'>&#x27;Humiliated, broken, powerless&#x27;: Sudan enters fourth year of war</a> - npr.org</li><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/04/15/2026/sudan-hunger-crisis-deepens-as-the-iran-war-sparks-a-fertilizer-shortage'>Sudan hunger crisis deepens as war enters fourth year</a> - semafor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/15/erosion-of-a-countrys-future-what-has-the-war-cost-sudan'>‘Erosion of a country’s future’: What has the war cost Sudan?</a